package main

// The gesture matrix — the protocol regression gate.
//
// A faithful port of tests/e2e/command_routine/gesture_matrix.py (novaflow),
// which drives a LIVE cell and asserts physical facts (joints measurably
// stop, IOs really fire). Here the same operator journeys run against the
// full in-process stack (real mux, real session engine, real NATS control
// bridge, novamock simulating rae's execution): "the robot moved" becomes
// "the simulated location advanced", everything else is the identical
// protocol. The live matrix remains the pre-release physical check; THIS
// suite is the every-commit proof that the protocol still works:
//
//	G1  pause mid-motion, then ←Back rewinds (not a no-op)
//	G2  pause then Resume continues to DONE           (over NATS — transport parity)
//	G3  Step→ advances exactly one command boundary
//	G4  a live speed change takes effect without restarting
//	G5  ←Back onto an IO point HOLDS for a decision; motion refused meanwhile
//	G6  seek is motion-only — the robot moves, no IO fires
//	G7  Halt during motion stops the robot and frees the claim
//	G8  an abandoned session faults even at standstill (deadman;
//	    the mid-run variant lives in TestIntegrationDeadmanOverTheWire)

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"math"
	"testing"
	"time"
)
--- e2e full_vocabulary
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The parts of the vocabulary the other live suites do not reach.
The OEM matrix runs one routine on many robots, which makes it broad and
shallow: PTP, a line, an arc, controller I/O. This one runs many routines on
one robot, and covers what that leaves out — every piece exercised against a
real cell rather than a mock:
  * a cubic spline through control points that name their own frame
  * a pause gate on I/O, and the supervised session it is meant for
  * fieldbus I/O, on a virtual PROFINET the suite stands up itself
  * a wait on a cell-driven input, which a robot's own output cannot answer
  * the re-teach property: move a frame, replan the same routine unchanged
Setup is the bootstrap's job (`bootstrap_cell.py`); this suite adds only the
fieldbus, which is cell equipment rather than robot state, and says so.
    NOVA_API_URL=https://<instance>/api/v2 RESOLVER_URL=http://localhost:18180 \
        python3 tests/full_vocabulary.py
"""
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(HERE), "lib"))
--- e2e configured_routines
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Live e2e for configured motion routines: real applications, not toy moves.
Four routines a cell would actually run, written the way a motion-authoring
client emits them — every cartesian target a point that names its frame, its
tool and the arm configuration that reaches it:
  palletize     pick from the conveyor, place a 2x3 grid on the pallet — the
                points live in two different frames, which is the case a
                joint-space program cannot express at all
  tend_fixture  approach, insert along the fixture's own tilted z, clamp,
                retract: the tilt is why the points are taught in the fixture
                frame and not in world
  seal_bead     a sealant bead around a part: line segments, an arc through a
                control point, tool on and off, blending
  inspect       a joint-space transit between two cartesian inspection poses
                in different frames
and two properties that are the reason the format exists:
  RE-TEACH      move the pallet frame (bump it, re-teach it) and re-plan the
                same routine unchanged: every point taught in that frame must
                move with it, exactly, and nothing taught elsewhere may move
  ONE POINT     the same physical location expressed in two different frames
                must resolve to the same world pose
